course: dictionary
lesson: 5158
title: Irregularity: when the pattern breaks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An irregularity is something that is not normal, even or regular. It can be a bump on a smooth road, a gap in a row of bricks, or a beat that is not steady. The word is made of ir- (not) and regular (following a pattern), so it means "not regular".
explain: Look at «The wall had an irregularity: one gap was much wider than the others.» The gap breaks the pattern of the bricks. Test your meaning by swapping in "uneven" or "not following a pattern". If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: In official reports the word has a second, serious meaning. «The report found irregularities in the accounts.» Here irregularities means things that are wrong or against the rules, often involving money. Officials sometimes choose long, paper-sounding words, so when you see one, ask what it really means.
fact: Irregularity means something that is not normal, even or regular.
fact: The prefix ir- means not, so irregular means not regular.
fact: In a formal report, «irregularities» often means wrongdoing, such as fraud (cheating people out of money).
fact: Officials may use paperwork words: fraud becomes "irregularities", and stealing money becomes "misappropriation of funds".
fact: Check the context: a bumpy road has an irregularity in its surface, but irregularities in a report are a warning that rules may have been broken.
exercise: «The road had an irregularity: a deep bump in the middle.» What is the irregularity here? | *Something uneven that breaks the smooth pattern | A road with a nice view of the hills | A road with too many cars on it | A straight, smooth road with no bumps
exercise: «The report found irregularities in the vote count.» What might irregularities mean in this news report? | A new, faster voting machine | A sunny afternoon at the polling station | *Something wrong or against the rules, such as cheating in the count | A list of names in alphabetical order
exercise: Officials say "irregularities" instead of "fraud". Why might they choose that word? | It means that nothing bad happened at all | It is a shorter word than fraud | It means exactly the same as a birthday party, so it is always happy | *It is a long, formal word that sounds less harsh than fraud
test: Which best shows an irregularity in a row of fence posts? | The posts are made of wood | Every post is the same height and the same distance apart | The fence is painted bright red | *One gap is much bigger than the others, so the spacing is uneven
test: Which best describes «irregularities» in an official report? | A pretty picture of the town hall | A poem about the weather | A list of the staff's birthdays | *Possible wrongdoing, such as cheating with money
exam: In «The report found irregularities in the accounts», what does irregularities most likely mean? | Extra days off for the staff | Pictures of the accounts office | *Wrongdoing, such as money going missing without a proper reason | A list of the accounts in neat columns
